  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "A professional roof inspection in Utah typically costs between $100 and $400, with the statewide average landing around $230 for a standard residential home. However, the exact price depends heavily on the type of inspection you choose, the purpose of the report, and the contractor you hire.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "between $100 and $400"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "\ud83d\udee0\ufe0f Cost by Inspection Type"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Physical Walk-On ($75 \u2013 $200): A traditional visual assessment where an inspector climbs onto easily accessible, lower-pitch roofs to look for missing shingles, wear, or storm damage .",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Drone Inspection ($150 \u2013 $400): Often utilized for steep, multi-story, or unstable roofs. Drones provide high-resolution photographic proof without the safety risk.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Infrared / Thermal Imaging ($400 \u2013 $700): Specialized inspections that identify heat patterns and trapped moisture under the shingles, revealing leaks that aren't visible to the naked eye."
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          1,
          2
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "\u2696\ufe0f Free Estimates vs. Paid Inspections"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Many local roofing contractors throughout the state (such as Homer Roofing or Ensign Roofing ) offer free roof assessments if you are checking for storm damage or need a quote for an upcoming replacement.",
        "reference_indexes": [
          3,
          4
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "However, you will usually have to pay a flat fee if:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "You need a Roof Certification ($75 \u2013 $200): A official document required by some mortgage lenders or insurance companies verifying the roof's structural integrity and remaining lifespan.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "You are buying/selling a home: Standard real estate home buyers' inspections usually wrap the roof into a general property inspection or charge a fee for a standalone trade report."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you are dealing with potential wind or hail damage, a user on Facebook noted that while formal physical reports can start around $500 from select providers, the majority of local roofers will readily provide free multi-point inspections and insurance estimates to get your business.",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "Facebook",
            "link": "https://www.facebook.com/groups/1219634021392380/posts/28176288521966898/"
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          5
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like to narrow down the cost, let me know:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "What is the purpose of the inspection? (Buying a house, insurance claim, or general leak tracking?)"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Approximately how old or large is the roof?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "What city or area in Utah are you located in?"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
